---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import '../../styles/global.css';
---

<Layout title="QryptChat · Aravind V. A." description="A secure chat application implementing the B92 quantum key distribution protocol, built during the IISc Quantum Computing & AI programme.">
  <a href="/#projects" class="proj-back">← Projects</a>

  <article>
    <section class="proj-hero reveal">
      <div class="proj-eyebrow">
        <span class="proj-eyebrow-num">01</span>
        <span>Quantum · Cryptography</span>
        <span>2025</span>
      </div>
      <h1 class="proj-title" transition:name="title-qryptchat">QryptChat</h1>
      <p class="proj-subtitle">
        A secure two-party chat application demonstrating the B92 quantum key distribution
        protocol — built as part of the IISc Quantum Computing &amp; AI certification programme.
      </p>
    </section>

    <div class="proj-content">

      <section class="proj-section reveal">
        <span class="proj-section-label">Overview</span>
        <div class="proj-section-body">
          <p>
            QryptChat demonstrates end-to-end secure communication using quantum key distribution (QKD)
            rather than classical cryptography. Unlike RSA or Diffie-Hellman, which rely on the
            computational difficulty of factoring large numbers, QKD derives its security from the
            laws of quantum mechanics — specifically, the no-cloning theorem and the observer effect.
          </p>
          <p>
            The project was built as the capstone for the Quantum Computing &amp; Artificial Intelligence
            certification at the Indian Institute of Science (IISc), Bengaluru (Nov 2025). It implements
            the B92 protocol — a two-state QKD scheme proposed by Bennett in 1992 as a simplification
            of the original BB84 protocol.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="proj-section reveal">
        <span class="proj-section-label">B92 Protocol</span>
        <div class="proj-section-body">
          <div class="proj-steps">
            <div class="proj-step">
              <span class="proj-step-num">01</span>
              <div class="proj-step-content">
                <h4>Key Generation</h4>
                <p>Alice encodes random bits using two non-orthogonal quantum states. In B92, only two states are used (vs. four in BB84), making implementation simpler. Each bit is transmitted as a quantum state through a quantum channel.</p>
              </div>
            </div>
            <div class="proj-step">
              <span class="proj-step-num">02</span>
              <div class="proj-step-content">
                <h4>Measurement</h4>
                <p>Bob measures each received qubit using a randomly chosen basis. When Bob's basis matches the non-orthogonal state Alice sent, he gets a definitive result; otherwise the result is inconclusive and those bits are discarded.</p>
              </div>
            </div>
            <div class="proj-step">
              <span class="proj-step-num">03</span>
              <div class="proj-step-content">
                <h4>Sifting</h4>
                <p>Alice and Bob publicly compare which bits produced conclusive results (without revealing the bit values). These form the sifted key — a shared secret that has never been transmitted classically.</p>
              </div>
            </div>
            <div class="proj-step">
              <span class="proj-step-num">04</span>
              <div class="proj-step-content">
                <h4>Eavesdrop Detection</h4>
                <p>Any eavesdropper (Eve) must measure the qubits to learn the key, but measuring a quantum state disturbs it. Alice and Bob detect Eve's presence by comparing a subset of their key bits — a high error rate signals interception.</p>
              </div>
            </div>
            <div class="proj-step">
              <span class="proj-step-num">05</span>
              <div class="proj-step-content">
                <h4>Secure Messaging</h4>
                <p>Once a secure key is established, it is used as a one-time pad to encrypt messages between the two parties. The quantum-derived key ensures that the encryption is information-theoretically secure.</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="proj-section reveal">
        <span class="proj-section-label">Context</span>
        <div class="proj-section-body">
          <p>
            The implementation was built on a simulated quantum channel — real quantum hardware
            (superconducting qubits, photonic systems) introduces noise, decoherence, and channel
            loss that make practical QKD significantly harder. The simulation demonstrates the protocol
            mechanics and eavesdropper detection faithfully, making it a useful educational artefact for
            understanding why QKD is considered unconditionally secure.
          </p>
          <p>
            This project sits at the intersection of two areas I find most compelling: quantum algorithms
            that exploit physical properties for computation, and the question of what cryptographic
            security looks like in a post-quantum world.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="proj-section reveal">
        <span class="proj-section-label">Stack</span>
        <div class="proj-section-body">
          <div class="proj-stack">
            <span class="proj-stack-item">Python</span>
            <span class="proj-stack-item">Qiskit</span>
            <span class="proj-stack-item">B92 QKD</span>
            <span class="proj-stack-item">Quantum Simulation</span>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="proj-section reveal">
        <span class="proj-section-label">Code</span>
        <div class="proj-section-body">
          <div class="proj-links">
            <a href="https://github.com/spearpx/qryptchat" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="proj-link">
              GitHub ↗
            </a>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

    </div>
  </article>
</Layout>

<script>
  import '../../scripts/main.js';
</script>
